FAQ = [["When did GPT-6.1 Sol launch in GitHub Copilot?","GitHub announced general availability and rollout on September 29, 2026. Availability in a specific Copilot client depends on the currently supported models and relevant account policies."],["How can I select GPT-6.1 Sol in VS Code?","Open GitHub Copilot Chat and inspect the available models in the picker. Update the Copilot client, check plan eligibility and ensure the organization has not restricted the model."],["Why is GPT-6.1 Sol missing from my Copilot model picker?","Possible causes include account entitlement, client or chat mode support, organizational model policies, staged rollout or a later model lifecycle change. Check GitHub's current supported-models list."],["Does GitHub Copilot charge OpenAI API token prices for GPT-6.1 Sol?","No. Copilot uses GitHub's own subscription, premium-request and model-multiplier rules. OpenAI API pricing applies separately when using an OpenAI API account."],["Is GPT-6.1 Sol intended for agentic coding and terminal tasks?","GitHub's announcement highlights agentic coding and terminal workflows. Actual tool access and permissions depend on the specific Copilot client and the application's authorization configuration."],["Is GPT-6.1 Sol objectively the best coding model in Copilot?","There is no single task-independent answer. Compare accepted patches, tests, tool-use reliability, latency, manual review time and actual usage on representative tasks."],["Can administrators disable GPT-6.1 Sol for their organization?","GitHub Copilot organizations and enterprises can manage model access under their policies. Ask an authorized administrator to verify the effective model settings."],["Is the Copilot model picker the same as OpenAI API access?","No. Model availability through GitHub Copilot is a separate product entitlement from direct usage through an OpenAI API key."]]
BODY = "<section class=\"signal-deep-dive shell\" data-gpt61-copilot-guide aria-labelledby=\"gpt61-copilot-guide\">\n<div class=\"intel-section-head\"><div><p class=\"eyebrow\">SXF TECHNICAL GUIDE / VERIFIED PRIMARY SOURCES</p><h2 id=\"gpt61-copilot-guide\">GPT-6.1 Sol in GitHub Copilot: availability, setup, costs and coding workflows.</h2></div><span>Updated October 31, 2026</span></div>\n<div class=\"deep-dive-lead\"><p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> GitHub announced GPT-6.1 Sol in GitHub Copilot on September 29, 2026, describing a generally available model rollout for agentic coding and terminal-based development. <strong>Model availability is not the same as universal access:</strong> the Copilot plan, product surface, organization policy and current model picker determine whether a particular user can select it. Use GitHub's current supported-model documentation to verify eligibility, rather than treating the original rollout announcement as a permanent compatibility guarantee.</p><p>This guide separates <strong>Copilot product access</strong> from <strong>OpenAI API access</strong>, explains safe setup and troubleshooting, and provides reproducible test ideas. It does not claim that the model beats competing models without independent, controlled measurements.</p></div>\n<nav aria-label=\"On this page\" class=\"deep-dive-section\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">CONTENTS</p><p><a href=\"#sol-copilot-availability\">Availability</a> · <a href=\"#sol-copilot-setup\">Setup</a> · <a href=\"#sol-copilot-workflows\">Workflows</a> · <a href=\"#sol-copilot-cost\">Pricing</a> · <a href=\"#sol-copilot-troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</a> · <a href=\"#sol-copilot-faq\">FAQ</a></p></nav>\n<div class=\"deep-dive-section\" id=\"sol-copilot-availability\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">RELEASE &amp; ELIGIBILITY</p><h2>Is GPT-6.1 Sol available in GitHub Copilot?</h2><p><strong>At announcement time:</strong> GitHub said the OpenAI model was generally available and rolling out in GitHub Copilot, with an emphasis on agentic coding and terminal workflows. The phrase <em>rolling out</em> matters: it describes availability being introduced across supported surfaces, not proof that every subscriber instantly sees the model in every editor.</p><p><strong>For access today:</strong> check the model in your actual Copilot model selector and GitHub's <a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/ai-models/supported-models\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">supported-models reference</a>. GitHub may revise models, client support, plan eligibility and deprecations after an announcement. If your organization controls model availability, administrators can restrict access regardless of a public release.</p><div class=\"deep-dive-facts\"><div><strong>Official announcement</strong><p>September 29, 2026 — GitHub Copilot model availability announcement. Read the <a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-09-29-gpt-6-1-sol-in-github-copilot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">original GitHub changelog</a>.</p></div><div><strong>Model identity</strong><p>GPT-6.1 Sol is a named OpenAI model. A Copilot model-picker selection is not the same thing as using an OpenAI API key or a model identifier in the OpenAI API.</p></div><div><strong>Access checks</strong><p>Look for the model in your particular Copilot client, verify plan eligibility and confirm any organization or enterprise AI-model policy.</p></div><div><strong>Lifecycle</strong><p>Model lists are dynamic. Confirm that this model has not been disabled, replaced or retired in the supported-models and deprecation documentation.</p></div></div></div>\n<div class=\"deep-dive-section\" id=\"sol-copilot-setup\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">PRACTICAL SETUP</p><h2>How to select GPT-6.1 Sol in Copilot Chat or a coding agent.</h2><ol class=\"deep-dive-checklist\"><li><strong>Verify entitlement.</strong> Sign in with the GitHub account that owns your Copilot entitlement. In organization-managed environments, confirm which enterprise or organization provides the license.</li><li><strong>Update your client.</strong> Use a supported version of VS Code, Visual Studio, JetBrains, GitHub.com or the Copilot CLI as applicable. Model picker options and agent features are not necessarily identical across clients.</li><li><strong>Open Copilot Chat.</strong> Look for the model selector. Choose <strong>GPT-6.1 Sol</strong> if it appears and is enabled for your account, client and conversation mode. Availability may differ between Ask, Edit and agent workflows.</li><li><strong>Check admin policies.</strong> If the model is absent from the picker on an organization-managed account, ask an authorized admin to review the organization's Copilot policies and model permissions rather than trying to bypass restrictions.</li><li><strong>Test a controlled task.</strong> Start with an existing test suite, a small repository change and a narrow scoped prompt. Review the proposed diff and run verification before accepting edits.</li></ol><p class=\"deep-dive-note\">The steps above describe how to inspect and use a model when it is offered by your current Copilot installation; they are <strong>not</strong> a guarantee that GPT-6.1 Sol is exposed in every interface. Use GitHub's <a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/how-tos/use-ai-models/change-the-chat-model\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">model-selection help</a> and <a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/ai-models/model-hosting\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">model availability information</a> for current differences.</p></div>\n<div class=\"deep-dive-section\" id=\"sol-copilot-workflows\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">DEVELOPER VALUE</p><h2>Four coding workflows worth testing with GPT-6.1 Sol.</h2><div class=\"deep-dive-grid\"><article><span>01 / MULTI-FILE CHANGE</span><h3>Plan a cross-file refactor.</h3><p>Give the agent the affected module boundaries, style conventions and tests. Ask for a plan first, then implement one scoped commit. Review file changes, type checks and regression tests rather than treating a confident explanation as evidence of correctness.</p></article><article><span>02 / TERMINAL WORKFLOW</span><h3>Debug with a reproducible command.</h3><p>Share a failing test command, expected result and relevant logs without secrets. Ask for the smallest diagnostic next step. Require explicit approval for commands that edit files, contact services, alter dependencies or affect production infrastructure.</p></article><article><span>03 / PR REVIEW</span><h3>Find a concrete regression risk.</h3><p>Supply a diff and ask which branches of logic were changed, what assumptions might break, and which tests would detect it. Treat suggestions as hypotheses to verify, especially on security-sensitive code paths.</p></article><article><span>04 / AGENT HANDOFF</span><h3>Keep state across a larger task.</h3><p>Write an objective, constraints, acceptance criteria and a short running change log. Verify that the selected Copilot surface has the required repository permissions and context; model capability alone does not grant access to tools or files.</p></article></div><p><strong>Example evaluation prompt:</strong> “Fix the failing integration test without changing the public API. First identify the root cause, then propose the smallest patch and two additional regression cases. Do not modify dependencies or run destructive commands without approval. Report which tests actually ran.” Compare multiple models on the same repository snapshot and accepted tests, not on impressions alone.</p></div>\n<div class=\"deep-dive-section\" id=\"sol-copilot-cost\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">BILLING &amp; MODEL COMPARISONS</p><h2>Does GPT-6.1 Sol cost extra in GitHub Copilot?</h2><p>Do not apply OpenAI's standalone API token prices to a GitHub Copilot subscription. Copilot usage is governed by <strong>GitHub's own plan entitlements, premium requests and model-specific usage multipliers</strong>, which GitHub can change. Review the <a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/billing/copilot-requests\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Copilot request billing explanation</a> and the <a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/ai-models/supported-models\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">currently supported-models table</a> before estimating quota or overage charges.</p><div class=\"deep-dive-table-wrap\"><table class=\"deep-dive-table\"><caption>Different product surfaces have different billing and access rules</caption><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>GitHub Copilot</th><th>OpenAI API</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Who manages access?</th><td>GitHub plans, client support and organization policies</td><td>OpenAI API account, project and supported API endpoint</td></tr><tr><th>What determines cost?</th><td>Copilot plan and applicable premium-request / multiplier policy</td><td>Published API units and usage for the exact supported model</td></tr><tr><th>Can you assume prices transfer?</th><td>No — Copilot's billing contract is separate</td><td>No — API pricing is not Copilot quota pricing</td></tr><tr><th>Where to verify?</th><td>Copilot supported-models and billing documentation</td><td><a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-6.1-sol\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official OpenAI model card</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/pricing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">API pricing</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>No unverified premium-request multiplier or per-task cost is presented here. Your plan, selected mode and the current GitHub price schedule determine actual chargeable use.</p></div>\n<div class=\"deep-dive-section\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">MODEL CHOICE</p><h2>When should a developer evaluate Sol against other Copilot models?</h2><p>Choose by workload, supported product surface and measurable outcomes. A model's vendor positioning as strong at multi-step coding is useful context, but it is <strong>not</strong> a controlled head-to-head result. Measure accepted patches, failed tests, latency, tool-use errors, required manual corrections and chargeable Copilot usage on the same tasks.</p><p>For broader context see SXF's <a href=\"/models/gpt-6-1-sol/\">GPT-6.1 Sol model reference</a>, <a href=\"/guides/best-ai-coding-tools/\">AI coding tools guide</a>, <a href=\"/guides/ai-agent-security/\">agent security guide</a> and <a href=\"/topics/github-copilot/\">GitHub Copilot news archive</a>. Keep separate the <strong>original GitHub Copilot release</strong> from <a href=\"/signals/introducing-gpt-6-1-sol-dd66aca/\">OpenAI's introduction of GPT-6.1 Sol</a>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"deep-dive-section\" id=\"sol-copilot-troubleshooting\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">TROUBLESHOOTING</p><h2>Why GPT-6.1 Sol might not appear in your model picker.</h2><ol class=\"deep-dive-checklist\"><li><strong>Wrong account or entitlement:</strong> confirm which signed-in GitHub identity owns the Copilot plan and whether your editor uses the same identity.</li><li><strong>Unsupported interface or mode:</strong> compare available models in the current product's picker with GitHub's documented support matrix.</li><li><strong>Organization restriction:</strong> an administrator may have disabled specific models or third-party model providers. Request a policy review rather than using a workaround.</li><li><strong>Client version or rollout:</strong> update your extension or application and review release notes; a historic GA announcement does not promise simultaneous availability in every product.</li><li><strong>Lifecycle change:</strong> check GitHub's deprecation notices and current model list if the option previously appeared and is now gone.</li></ol><p>For persistent access failures, record the client version, account type, plan, mode and exact displayed error before contacting GitHub Support. Avoid sharing repository credentials or private source code in public issue reports.</p></div>\n<div class=\"deep-dive-section\" id=\"sol-copilot-faq\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">READER FAQ</p><h2>GPT-6.1 Sol in GitHub Copilot — frequently asked questions.</h2><div class=\"deep-dive-faq\"><details><summary>When did GPT-6.1 Sol arrive in GitHub Copilot?</summary><p>GitHub announced general availability and rollout on September 29, 2026. Check the current Copilot supported-model list for present availability.</p></details><details><summary>Can I use GPT-6.1 Sol in VS Code?</summary><p>Check the Copilot Chat model selector in your installed VS Code client and GitHub's supported-model matrix. Availability depends on account, mode, client and organizational policy.</p></details><details><summary>Is GPT-6.1 Sol available for Copilot agent mode?</summary><p>GitHub's announcement highlights agentic coding, but support can differ by client and current model policy. Verify the model under the specific agent surface you intend to use.</p></details><details><summary>How do I enable GPT-6.1 Sol for an organization?</summary><p>Have an authorized Copilot administrator review model policies and eligible plans in GitHub settings. Developers should not bypass organization security controls.</p></details><details><summary>Does GPT-6.1 Sol use OpenAI API pricing inside Copilot?</summary><p>No. GitHub Copilot follows its own plan, premium request and multiplier rules. OpenAI API pricing applies separately to direct OpenAI API use.</p></details><details><summary>Why is GPT-6.1 Sol not listed in my Copilot model picker?</summary><p>Check account entitlement, current supported models, client version, mode, organization restrictions, and any GitHub model deprecation notices.</p></details><details><summary>Can GPT-6.1 Sol safely run terminal commands?</summary><p>No coding model makes terminal actions inherently safe. Use least privilege, approval gates, sandboxing and a human review of consequential commands or file edits.</p></details><details><summary>Is GPT-6.1 Sol faster or more accurate than another model?</summary><p>That depends on tasks, tool configuration and test environment. GitHub's release announcement is not an independent benchmark. Evaluate with identical prompts, checks and cost accounting.</p></details></div></div>\n<div class=\"deep-dive-sources\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">PRIMARY SOURCES &amp; DISCLOSURE</p><h2>Where the facts come from.</h2><p>Original announcement published September 29, 2026. Editorial context reviewed October 31, 2026. Provider policy and model availability may change; use live documentation when making a purchase or deployment decision. No claimed independent benchmark result is implied.</p><a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-09-29-gpt-6-1-sol-in-github-copilot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>GitHub Changelog</strong><span>Original GPT-6.1 Sol Copilot announcement ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/ai-models/supported-models\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>GitHub Docs — Supported models</strong><span>Current support, client availability and lifecycle ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/billing/copilot-requests\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>GitHub Docs — Copilot usage and billing</strong><span>Premium requests and model-based usage accounting ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/how-tos/use-ai-models/change-the-chat-model\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>GitHub Docs — Change the chat model</strong><span>Model selector help ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-6.1-sol\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>OpenAI — GPT-6.1 Sol API reference</strong><span>Separate API context, pricing and capabilities ↗</span></a></div>\n</section>"

